# Codex review of 332, item 2: `h: f` defines h by the function f (a new reference to it); `r: h(3)`, reached after it,
# applies the bound h once and defines r by its result, exactly as `s: f(3)` does. Nothing is decided before the
# traversal reaches a field.
fn: f (int: a) int
    return: a
end: f
@: h f
int: r h(3)
int: s f(3)
